WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.160
Windows forensics is the preservation and analysis of evidence from Windows systems.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:15.620
Those systems may be employee laptops, desktops, servers, virtual machines, cloud-hosted Windows instances, or specialized systems that support business operations.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:25.520
The purpose is to understand what happened, when it happened, what accounts or systems were involved, and what evidence supports those conclusions. Windows forensics is a core part of DFIR.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:35.100
It supports incident response, insider investigations, malware triage, policy investigations, user activity reconstruction, and breach analysis.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:46.120
A Windows system can contain evidence in event logs, user profiles, registry hives, file system metadata, execution traces, security tool telemetry, application data, and memory.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:52.100
The analyst's job is to preserve and correlate those sources, not to rely on a single artifact.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:01:05.600
Authorization and careful handling matter because Windows endpoints often contain sensitive business data, personal information, credentials, browser activity, chat history, source code, documents, and regulated records.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:17.660
Forensic collection should follow approved authority, case scope, and data handling rules. The analyst should know what is being collected, why it is needed, and how it will be protected. Evidence integrity is the foundation.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:28.200
Investigator actions can alter timestamps, create logs, change memory, or overwrite artifacts. Sometimes live response is necessary, but it should be deliberate and documented.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:39.940
An offline run should record its source type and preserve hashed mount-session and image-to-device mapping records, so later work can be tied to the verified mount. Strong Windows forensics is not about memorizing tools.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:47.920
It is about preserving context, interpreting artifacts carefully, building timelines, and communicating confidence and limitations clearly.
